“𝗖𝗮𝗻 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝗲𝘅𝗽𝗹𝗮𝗶𝗻 𝗵𝗼𝘄 𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗿𝗲𝗰𝗲𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗰𝗹𝗶𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗳𝗳𝗶𝗰?”

Most people say:
👉 “Through a Service.”

That answer works…
until you’re asked the follow-up:

“What actually happens when a client hits a load balancer pointing to your Kubernetes nodes?”

Here’s the real traffic path, step by step — 
⸻

What actually happens when a request hits Kubernetes

1️⃣ Client → External Load Balancer
This LB only knows about node IPs, not pods.

⸻

2️⃣ Load Balancer → NodePort
The LB forwards traffic to a NodePort opened by a Kubernetes Service.
Any healthy node can receive the request — even if no pod is running there.

⸻

3️⃣ Node → kube-proxy (iptables/IPVS)
kube-proxy rewrites the destination using iptables or IPVS rules.
It selects one of the Service’s backend pod IPs.

This is where:
	•	Session affinity
	•	Load balancing
	•	NAT decisions
actually happen.

⸻

4️⃣ Node → Pod (Cross-node routing)
If the chosen pod is on another node, traffic crosses the CNI network
(Calico / Cilium / Flannel / etc.).

If it’s local, it stays on the same node.

⸻

5️⃣ Pod → Container → Application
The packet finally reaches the container port.
Only now does your application see the request.

⸻

Why this matters in real systems

🔹 NodePort range misconfigured → traffic never reaches kube-proxy
🔹 kube-proxy failure → Service exists, traffic silently drops
🔹 CNI misrouting → random timeouts under load
🔹 ExternalTrafficPolicy=Local → dropped traffic if pod isn’t on that node
🔹 Health checks hitting nodes without pods → false negatives
🔹 Source IP lost → debugging and rate limiting break

If you don’t understand the path, you can’t debug the failure.

⸻

The mental model that actually works

Client → LB → NodePort → kube-proxy → Pod IP → Container

Not:
❌ Client → Service → Pod

Kubernetes abstracts it — but production exposes it.

⸻

Knowing this isn’t “nice to have.”
It’s the difference between deploying Kubernetes and operating it.
